  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 400 AM Wednesday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Scattered showers and perhaps a few storms are \npossible from Wednesday night into Thursday night. Temperatures will \nbe well above normal through this period.\n\nA broad mid/upper trough will approach from the west through \nThursday, with an embedded weakening shortwave that moves from the \nDeep South into the Southern Appalachians on Wednesday night. Deep-\nlayer moisture will increase with deep SW flow bringing in plenty of \nGulf moisture. PW values are modeled to be as much as 200-225% of \nnormal. This will result in a chance of showers beginning on \nWednesday evening/night, with the western Piedmont being the most \nfavored area, but the best forcing will still go to our west and \nnorth. So forecast rainfall amounts are less than a quarter inch. \n\nThere is also a chance of showers during the day Thursday, and \nisolated storms are possible given about 500-1000 J/kg of CAPE. But \ncoverage should be more isolated given a relative minimum in \nforcing. Shower and storm chances increase again late Thursday night \nas a more potent shortwave tracks into the lower MS Valley and a \nslow-moving cold front approaches from western NC.\n\nDue to the southerly flow ahead of the cold front, temperatures will \nbe very warm today and tomorrow, with highs in the mid 70s to lower \n80s (10-15 degrees above normal). Lows will be in the lower-to-mid-\n60s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... The chance of showers will maximize ahead of and \nalong a cold front on Fri, with a few thunderstorms possible \nespecially late morning through afternoon. \n\nThe models indicate a mid/upr-level cyclone now over the cntl Great \nBasin will progress ewd and reach the mid MS Valley and Mid-South by \nFriday morning, then pivot ewd and across the OH and TN Valleys \nFriday and the nrn Middle Atlantic and Northeast Fri night. Related \nmodest, 40-60 meter 500 mb height falls, and 35-45 kts of swly 500 \nmb flow, will overspread cntl NC on Fri, as the strongest flow and \nforcing for ascent otherwise pass just to the northwest and along \nthe cntl Appalachians. \n\nAt the surface, a synoptic cold front that will have slowed and \npossibly stalled over the southern Appalachians by early Friday will \nmove slowly ewd and across the srn Middle Atlantic and Carolinas \nthrough early Friday night, then offshore Sat morning. The front may \nbe preceded by a pre-frontal trough or differential heating/outflow \nboundary from elevated early morning convection Fri, which may serve \nas the principal focus for deeper, surface-based convection by late \nmorning-early afternoon. Mesoscale uncertainties regarding the \nlocation and extent of early morning convection will have \nramifications on where subsequent convection may develop and deepen \nwith diurnal heating later Fri, and where the corridor of heaviest \nrain may align. \n\nRegarding that rainfall potential, there has been a slight increase \nin QPF across physics-based and AI guidance and ensembles since 24 \nhours ago. Specifically, the 50th percentile of each in 00Z/4th \nguidance depict 0.5 to 0.75\" amounts, up by about a quarter inch and \nwhat may be considered the \"best guess\" at this time. 10th \npercentiles range from around 0.25\", while 90th percentiles range \nfrom 0.75 to 1.5\", highest in EPS and GEFS guidance and lowest in \ntheir AI counterparts. While the availability of hi-resolution, \nconvective allowing guidance may increase forecast confidence in \nmost likely amounts as time draws nearer, the aforementioned \nmesoscale uncertainties will probably remain through Fri morning. \n\nValues in forecast instability fields are also slightly higher \nrelative to 24 hours ago, which suggests a greater potential for \nthunder mainly with the expected deeper convection from late morning \nthrough afternoon Friday. With slightly greater instability of ~750 \nJ/kg forecast, and amid strengthening swly flow aloft, a low-end \nrisk for strong to locally damaging wind gusts may result.",
